<title>GERMANY: Important mark remains strong for EMU - Tietmeyer.</title>
<headline>Important mark remains strong for EMU - Tietmeyer.</headline>
<dateline>ASCHAU, Germany 1997-07-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer said on Thursday it was important that the German mark remain strong going into the start of European monetary union.</p>
<p>Tietmeyer also told a news conference following a fortnightly council meeting that foreign exchange market developments have helped brighten prospects for growth and that inflation was largely under control for the foreseeable future.</p>
<p>&quot;At the moment we do not see any inflation impulses,&quot; Tietmeyer said.</p>
<p>He also said, &quot;We will naturally watch the developments in markets. You can be sure that it is important for us that the mark remains a strong currency and enter the euro as a strong currency.&quot;</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom +49 69 756525</p>
